1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,385
Imagine an agent places an order.

2
00:00:03,164 --> 00:00:06,364
The service creates it, but
the response disappears.

3
00:00:07,129 --> 00:00:09,644
The agent sees a timeout and tries again.

4
00:00:10,111 --> 00:00:13,362
The engineering question is whether
that second attempt recovers the

5
00:00:13,375 --> 00:00:15,617
first order or creates another one.

6
00:00:16,182 --> 00:00:20,048
Today, I will make that distinction
observable with a small public

7
00:00:20,069 --> 00:00:24,777
MCP experiment, then extend it
into a practical chaos test plan.

8
00:00:25,544 --> 00:00:27,641
The experiment uses synthetic effects.

9
00:00:28,283 --> 00:00:32,207
It is an illustration of a familiar
distributed systems boundary,

10
00:00:32,879 --> 00:00:36,973
not evidence of an incident at
an employer or a deployed vendor.

11
00:00:37,762 --> 00:00:39,661
I'm speaking in my personal capacity.

12
00:00:40,359 --> 00:00:44,955
The useful outcome is a recovery
policy that follows evidence about

13
00:00:44,986 --> 00:00:49,208
effects, even when the caller has
very little evidence about the reply.

14
00:00:50,208 --> 00:00:51,853
Keep the two perspectives separate.

15
00:00:52,386 --> 00:00:54,769
The service knows it appended a record.

16
00:00:55,249 --> 00:00:59,142
The caller knows that a response
did not arrive before a deadline.

17
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:02,175
Neither observation cancels the other.

18
00:01:02,884 --> 00:01:07,137
A system that turns every timeout
into a new business operation

19
00:01:07,499 --> 00:01:11,401
makes a consequential policy
choice using incomplete evidence.

20
00:01:12,562 --> 00:01:16,746
This is why increasing the timeout,
improving the prompt, or telling

21
00:01:16,748 --> 00:01:20,061
the model to be careful cannot
close this failure boundary

22
00:01:21,061 --> 00:01:23,421
Those changes may alter its frequency.

23
00:01:23,924 --> 00:01:28,609
They do not establish what happened
during a particular ambiguous attempt.

24
00:01:29,427 --> 00:01:30,659
Keep one question in mind.

25
00:01:32,104 --> 00:01:37,159
What one new evidence would make
the next mutation justified?

26
00:01:37,976 --> 00:01:42,967
That question will lead us to
operational identity, authoritative

27
00:01:42,969 --> 00:01:44,781
read back, and bounded recovery

28
00:01:45,781 --> 00:01:48,196
Follow the order across the two lifelines.

29
00:01:48,694 --> 00:01:53,670
Attempt A reaches the service,
and the handler records effect E1.

30
00:01:54,221 --> 00:01:58,261
At that point, the order exists
in the experiment's ledger.

31
00:01:59,099 --> 00:02:02,799
The reply then disappears
before the caller receives it.

32
00:02:03,333 --> 00:02:08,114
The caller reaches its timeout
while E1 rema-remains recorded.

33
00:02:09,041 --> 00:02:11,765
Now, the application sends attempt B.

34
00:02:12,282 --> 00:02:15,935
Without duplicate suppression,
the service can append E2.

35
00:02:16,632 --> 00:02:20,593
The failure in the evidence path has
become an additional business effect.

36
00:02:21,115 --> 00:02:26,161
In this experiment, we can establish
the ordering because the relay drops

37
00:02:26,168 --> 00:02:28,709
the reply after the handler returns.

38
00:02:29,142 --> 00:02:31,854
A real caller may have
much less visibility.

39
00:02:32,238 --> 00:02:36,370
That is why its recovery policy must
resolve the possibility that the

40
00:02:36,406 --> 00:02:38,608
original operation already completed

41
00:02:39,608 --> 00:02:41,713
These details constrain the claim.

42
00:02:42,188 --> 00:02:47,111
The reproduction uses a real SDK client
